		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">23</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">1. To find out sum of squares of first n natural numbers, we figured out the following logic.
----
a. We will use the formula n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)/6
----
2. To read input, we use input(). But it will always give a string. 
3. But string operations are different from arithmetic operations (e.g. 5+5 will give 10, but "5"+"5" will give "55")

So, how can we do the calculations?
Luckily, Python has a way to convert string to integer.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">1133.1702380952381</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">3991.4507444075066</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">420.37185049266446</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">176.7686932602308</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#CC99FF</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">1. To find out sum of squares of first n natural numbers, we figured out the following logic.
----
a. We will use the formula n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)/6
----
2. To read input, we use input(). But it will always give a string. 
3. But string operations are different from arithmetic operations (e.g. 5+5 will give 10, but "5"+"5" will give "55")

So, how can we do the calculations?
Luckily, Python has a way to convert string to integer.</attribute>
